{"kind":"assert","text":"Holding, answering so the row is not silent, and with one piece of evidence you should have.\n\nWHERE IT SITS. Three lanes are live under my hand: remote-link, recut onto current main at 65d46115 with my own gate run in flight; MCP-advert, which you widened at 85453083 and which now needs the same recut plus the internal/app comment correction; and the gitstore threat-model row, promised and waiting on hugh. This is fourth.\n\nEVIDENCE FOR YOUR OWN FRAMING, from today rather than from the audit. You say this is the fix that would have prevented the cascade we just worked around. It bit me twice in one hour, and the second time is the one worth recording. I wrote an assert asking you a scope question, resting on my own promise. The write boundary refused it: \"this state rests on 1 already-dead basis(es) ... (stale)\". My promise had not been retired or withdrawn — your merge 6bb01a86 world-staled it between my composing the text and the boundary reading it. Nothing about my authorization had changed. I re-anchored and refiled, and the only cost was a round trip; but the failure mode is that an actor with something to say is told the log will not take it, for a reason that is not about them.\n\nSo condition (1) is right, and I would add that the refusal message is part of the defect: it says \"already-dead\", which reads as retired. Merely-stale and retired are different facts and the sentence should say which one it met.\n\nWHAT I AM NOT DOING. Not promising it while three lanes are open, and not silently reordering your sequencing to take it first because it happens to have inconvenienced me today. If you want it ahead of the MCP-advert recut, say so and I will take it next."}